  "body": "### What problem are you trying to solve?\n\nTo provide alternative text for an animated image sequence, you can use the `alt` attribute:\n\n```html\n<img src=\"clip.gif\" alt=\"Old man yells at cloud\">\n```\n\nThese days, video is often preferable. The quality is higher, the file size is smaller, and the viewer gains playback controls. You can achieve similar behavior to animated GIFs through a combination of preferences:\n\n```html\n<video controls autoplay loop muted playsinline src=\"clip.mp4\"></video>\n```\n\nBut there isn't a straightforward replacement for the `alt` attribute. (This has been the most common point of confusion in response to [a talk on modern-day GIF-like animations I presented last year](https://cloudfour.com/presents/gifs-are-forever-lets-make-them-better/).)\n\nAnimated GIFs may or may not [be declining in popularity](https://www.theatlantic.com/technology/archive/2022/10/using-reaction-gifs-over-tumblr-giphy/671680/), but this need would also apply to more modern short-form video content (the style popularized by TikTok, YouTube Shorts, etc.). These clips are short in length, often with text-based captions that would make them comparable to a GIF or meme in terms of summarization needs.\n\n### What solutions exist today?\n\nSome authors [incorrectly assume fallback content will work](https://stackoverflow.com/a/63614226), but that only applies to browsers that don't support `<video>`.\n\nAnother common suggestion is to use [captions and subtitles](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/Media/Guides/Audio_and_video_delivery/Adding_captions_and_subtitles_to_HTML5_video), but those are comparatively time-consuming to generate, and are intended to accompany the media rather than provide a complete alternative.\n\nThe most concise and `alt`-like current solution is to use `aria-label` ([or `aria-description`](https://urbanists.social/@georgelund/114116882822870800)):\n\n```html\n<video controls autoplay loop muted playsinline\n  aria-label=\"Old man yells at cloud\"\n  src=\"clip.mp4\"></video>\n```\n\nBut this is only reliable [when the `controls` attribute is present](https://www.tpgi.com/short-note-on-aria-label-aria-labelledby-and-aria-describedby/). It may also [run afoul of automated translation](https://adrianroselli.com/2019/11/aria-label-does-not-translate.html).\n\nYou could replace that with `aria-labelledby` ([or `aria-describedby`](https://urbanists.social/@georgelund/114116882822870800)):\n\n```html\n<video controls autoplay loop muted playsinline\n  aria-labelledby=\"video-label\"\n  src=\"clip.mp4\"></video>\n<div id=\"video-label\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\n  Old man yells at cloud\n</div>\n```\n\nBut in addition to requiring the `controls` attribute as before, you now need a separate element with a corresponding ID, and you'll likely need to manage the extra element's visibility somehow.\n\nThe simplest solution may be a figure and caption:\n\n```html\n<figure>\n  <video controls autoplay loop muted playsinline src=\"clip.mp4\"></video>\n  <figcaption>Old man yells at cloud</figcaption>\n</figure>\n```\n\nBut this introduces two new elements, and may require visibility of the caption to be managed.\n\nSome browsers support video in `<img>` elements:\n\n```html\n<img src=\"clip.mp4\" alt=\"Old man yells at cloud\">\n```\n\nBut aside from inconsistent support, this requires the author choose between supplying alternative text or providing playback controls.\n\n### How would you solve it?\n\nI would include the `alt` attribute as a feature of `<video>`:\n\n```html\n<video controls autoplay loop muted playsinline\n  alt=\"Old man yells at cloud\"\n  src=\"clip.mp4\"></video>\n```\n\nFor consistency with #6627, this could also apply to individual `<source>` elements:\n\n```html\n<video controls loop muted playsinline>\n  <source src=\"clip.mp4\" type=\"video/mp4\"\n    alt=\"Old man yells at cloud\" />\n</video>\n```\n\nAnd it would be great for the feature to play nicely with #7954, so there's clarity when `alt` text applies to poster images versus the media itself.\n\n### Anything else?\n\n_No response_",
